But look at it this way.

57
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:04,470
Every challenge is an opportunity.

58
00:02:05,230 --> 00:02:07,910
And anyway, I'm not talking about investing in the domestic market.

59
00:02:08,350 --> 00:02:10,190
There are emerging markets that promise great returns.

60
00:02:10,850 --> 00:02:11,690
Look at China, for example.

61
00:02:12,190 --> 00:02:16,070
They have 1.4 billion people, half a billion of whom have recently entered the middle class.

62
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:21,370
Here alone, the aggregate demand for consumer goods represents an amazing wealth-generating opportunity.

63
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:24,410
Oh, come on, son, you're looking at this too naively.

64
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:31,050
The Chinese market has exhibited a great deal of instability, and their currency has been devalued by almost a whole percentage point.

65
00:02:31,730 --> 00:02:32,270
Fine, then.

66
00:02:32,610 --> 00:02:34,230
If that's the way you feel, so be it.

67
00:02:34,470 --> 00:02:36,290
But you're losing out on a great opportunity here.

68
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:49,720
I'm going to hit up mom for the cash.

69
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:53,910
Well, it sounds like the dad has missed out on a really big investment opportunity, hey?

70
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:55,910
Yeah, and his son is pretty smart.

71
00:02:57,440 --> 00:02:58,250
Pretty smart little boy.

72
00:02:59,070 --> 00:03:02,390
Okay, so we have some great words that we want to look at here.

73
00:03:02,970 --> 00:03:05,330
Why don't we start with the first one, turmoil.

74
00:03:05,890 --> 00:03:06,290
Turmoil.

75
00:03:07,050 --> 00:03:07,530
Turmoil.

76
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:08,920
Turmoil.

77
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:12,290
So we have some great examples of how turmoil is used in different sentences.

78
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:13,450
So let's listen.

79
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:24,010
Example one, with a civil war, a famine, and inflation, the country has been in turmoil for ten years.

80
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:36,930
Example two, there's been a lot of turmoil in my life recently.

81
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:38,840
I've moved to a new city.

82
00:03:38,930 --> 00:03:39,690
I've changed my job.

83
00:03:40,270 --> 00:03:41,310
I broke up with my girlfriend.

84
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:54,600
Example three, the devaluation of the dollar caused a great deal of turmoil in the markets.

85
00:03:58,380 --> 00:04:01,520
So turmoil is a state of...

86
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:04,940
Of uncertainty, of change, of difficult times.

87
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:05,920
A little bit of chaos.

88
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:07,600
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

89
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:13,580
So for example, when there's a lot of turmoil in the market, that means stock prices are changing drastically from day to day.

90
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:16,020
Right, or the economy is in turmoil.

91
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:18,340
There's a lot of problems going on.

92
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:19,760
Yeah, uncertainty.

93
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:20,300
Uncertainty.

94
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:21,760
Okay, good word.

95
00:04:22,080 --> 00:04:27,930
All right, and we also saw something really interesting in the dialogue about a proposed stimulus package.

96
00:04:28,320 --> 00:04:31,560
A proposed stimulus package.

97
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:35,150
So a proposed stimulus package is something that's going on now.

98
00:04:35,360 --> 00:04:36,670
Yes, let's break this phrase down.

99
00:04:37,130 --> 00:04:38,990
So we've got stimulus package.

100
00:04:39,450 --> 00:04:43,490
A stimulus package, something to stimulate the economy, right?

101
00:04:43,730 --> 00:04:46,710
Right, so money from the government to get the economy moving.

102
00:04:47,010 --> 00:04:48,530
To get it moving, stimulus package.

103
00:04:49,110 --> 00:04:49,550
Proposed.

104
00:04:50,410 --> 00:04:52,370
It means that it hasn't been approved yet.

105
00:04:52,650 --> 00:04:53,390
Right, it's suggested.

106
00:04:53,640 --> 00:04:54,830
It's suggested, it's an idea.

107
00:04:56,120 --> 00:05:01,160
So the proposed stimulus package for the US economy, for example, is $150 billion.

108
00:05:01,640 --> 00:05:07,480
Right, that's $150 billion of American taxpayers' money that the government wants to pay.

109
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:09,220
Put into the economy to keep it moving.

110
00:05:09,480 --> 00:05:10,340
To keep it moving, right.

111
00:05:10,940 --> 00:05:15,920
So if it works or not, that's still a debate, but it's a proposed stimulus package.

112
00:05:16,360 --> 00:05:19,060
Let's move to our third phrase, aggregate demand.

113
00:05:20,120 --> 00:05:21,040
Aggregate demand.

114
00:05:22,970 --> 00:05:23,580
Aggregate demand.

115
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:23,810
Aggregate demand.

116
00:05:24,580 --> 00:05:27,650
So demand is pretty clear, but this word aggregate, what's that about?

117
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:30,610
Aggregate is like a combined, added.

118
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:32,320
Yeah.

119
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:33,320
Right?

120
00:05:33,320 --> 00:05:34,320
Yeah.

121
00:05:34,320 --> 00:05:35,320
Aggregate demand.

122
00:05:35,320 --> 00:05:46,680
So like China, where cities like in Shanghai or cities like Beijing, where more and more people are accumulating wealth, the aggregate demand is increasing.

123
00:05:46,680 --> 00:05:47,680
Right, right.

124
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:50,370
The combined demand for consumer goods is growing.

125
00:05:50,920 --> 00:05:51,920
Right.

126
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:53,930
More people have more money, so they're spending more.

127
00:05:54,640 --> 00:05:55,640
Yes.

128
00:05:55,640 --> 00:05:56,640
Okay.

129
00:05:56,640 --> 00:05:59,720
You know, Marco, there's a ton of excellent language in this dialogue, isn't there?

130
00:05:59,960 --> 00:06:01,580
Yeah, a lot of good stuff here.

131
00:06:01,700 --> 00:06:01,880
